Here is what a complete, end-to-end webserver provisioning workflow looks like inside a Wintersys ADT build script (such as InstallWebserver.sh or BuildWebserver.sh), combining your nala S3 cache wrapper for OS packages with adt-source-cache.sh for source-compiled software and pre-built binaries.

Complete ADT Webserver Build Example (Nginx + Certbot)

In Wintersys ADT, webserver provisioning happens in four clean stages:
1. Runtime OS dependencies (installed on every node via your cached nala wrapper so binaries never fail with missing .so libraries).
2. Source-compiled webserver (cached via adt-source-cache.sh, so compilation only happens once per version/config).
3. Pre-built CLI binaries (cached via adt-source-cache.sh to protect against upstream outages).
4. System users, directories, and systemd service (applied locally so UIDs/permissions always match the host OS).
sh
#!/bin/sh


nala in this example is a symlink to the read through cache

###############################################################################################
# Description: Wintersys ADT Webserver Provisioning Script (Nginx compiled from source)
#              Uses S3 read-through caching for both APT packages and compiled artifacts
###############################################################################################
set -eu

ADT_BASE="${BUILD_HOME:-${HOME}}"
NGINX_VERSION="1.26.2"
NGINX_PREFIX="/usr/local/nginx"
LEGO_VERSION="4.20.4"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STEP 1: Install Runtime & Build Dependencies (via your S3-cached nala wrapper)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Runtime libraries MUST be installed on all nodes (even on a source cache HIT)
# Build tools (gcc, make, *-dev) ensure fallback compilation succeeds if S3 is ever down
nala update
nala install -y \
    ca-certificates \
    curl \
    tar \
    xz-utils \
    libpcre2-8-0 \
    libssl3 \
    zlib1g \
    build-essential \
    libpcre2-dev \
    libssl-dev \
    zlib1g-dev

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STEP 2: Build or Restore Nginx from S3 Source Cache
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Create the deterministic build recipe script (its contents are hashed into the S3 cache key)
BUILD_RECIPE="/tmp/build-nginx-${NGINX_VERSION}.sh"
cat <<'EOF' > "${BUILD_RECIPE}"
#!/bin/sh
set -eu
VER="$1"
PREFIX="$2"

SRC_DIR="$(mktemp -d /tmp/nginx-src.XXXXXX)"
trap 'rm -rf "${SRC_DIR}"' EXIT

cd "${SRC_DIR}"
curl -fsSL "https://nginx.org{VER}.tar.gz" -o nginx.tar.gz
tar -xzf nginx.tar.gz
cd "nginx-${VER}"

./configure \
    --prefix="${PREFIX}" \
    --sbin-path="${PREFIX}/sbin/nginx" \
    --conf-path="${PREFIX}/conf/nginx.conf" \
    --error-log-path=/var/log/nginx/error.log \
    --http-log-path=/var/log/nginx/access.log \
    --pid-path=/run/nginx.pid \
    --lock-path=/run/nginx.lock \
    --user=www-data \
    --group=www-data \
    --with-http_ssl_module \
    --with-http_v2_module \
    --with-http_v3_module \
    --with-http_realip_module \
    --with-http_stub_status_module \
    --with-threads \
    --with-file-aio

make -j"$(nproc)"
make install
EOF
chmod 0755 "${BUILD_RECIPE}"

# Execute through the S3 artifact cache wrapper
/usr/local/bin/adt-source-cache.sh \
    "nginx" \
    "${NGINX_VERSION}" \
    "${NGINX_PREFIX}" \
    "${BUILD_RECIPE}" "${NGINX_VERSION}" "${NGINX_PREFIX}"

rm -f "${BUILD_RECIPE}"

# Symlink binary into global PATH
ln -sf "${NGINX_PREFIX}/sbin/nginx" /usr/local/sbin/nginx

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STEP 3: Install Pre-Built ACME/SSL Binary (Lego) via the Same S3 Cache Wrapper
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
/usr/local/bin/adt-source-cache.sh \
    "lego" \
    "${LEGO_VERSION}" \
    "/opt/lego" \
    sh -c "
        ARCH=\$(dpkg --print-architecture)
        mkdir -p /opt/lego
        curl -fsSL \"https://github.com{LEGO_VERSION}/lego_v${LEGO_VERSION}_linux_\${ARCH}.tar.gz\" \
            | tar -xz -C /opt/lego lego
        chmod 0755 /opt/lego/lego
    "

ln -sf /opt/lego/lego /usr/local/bin/lego

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STEP 4: Configure Local Host Directories, Ownership & Systemd Unit
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Done outside the cached tarball so UIDs/GIDs always match the target machine
mkdir -p /var/log/nginx /var/cache/nginx /etc/nginx/conf.d
chown -R www-data:www-data /var/log/nginx /var/cache/nginx

cat <<EOF > /etc/systemd/system/nginx.service
[Unit]
Description=Nginx HTTP and Reverse Proxy Server (ADT Source Build)
After=network-online.target remote-fs.target nss-lookup.target
Wants=network-online.target

[Service]
Type=forking
PIDFile=/run/nginx.pid
ExecStartPre=${NGINX_PREFIX}/sbin/nginx -t
ExecStart=${NGINX_PREFIX}/sbin/nginx
ExecReload=/bin/kill -s HUP \$MAINPID
ExecStop=/bin/kill -s TERM \$MAINPID
PrivateTmp=true

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
EOF

systemctl daemon-reload
systemctl enable nginx
Use code with caution.

How This Behaves in Your ADT Lifecycle


Deployment Scenario	What Happens	Typical Execution Time
1st Build (Cold Cache)	nala pulls .debs from upstream & populates S3; adt-source-cache.sh compiles Nginx from source, tars /usr/local/nginx, hashes it, and pushes to S3.	~2–4 minutes
2nd+ Build / Auto-Scale Node (Warm Cache)	nala pulls .debs from S3; adt-source-cache.sh pulls nginx-1.26.2-<hash>.tar.gz from S3, verifies SHA-256, and unpacks it. Zero C compilation occurs.	~10–15 seconds
Config Flag Changed	You add --with-http_sub_module inside BUILD_RECIPE. CONFIG_HASH changes automatically, triggering a fresh compile and caching the new variant in S3.	~2–4 minutes (once)
S3 Datastore Outage	Both nala and adt-source-cache.sh log warnings and seamlessly build from upstream sources without failing the server deployment.	~2–4 minutes

